<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  NOTHING LEAVES THE DEVICE THROUGH THE BACKUP FRAMEWORK.

  This file exists because android:allowBackup="false" is not sufficient on its
  own. Android's own documentation states the limit: for apps running on and
  targeting Android 12 (API 31) or higher, allowBackup="false" disables
  cloud backup but "doesn't disable device-to-device transfers for the app".
  This build targets API 36, so it is squarely inside that range — the manifest
  attribute closes the cloud path and leaves the transfer path open.

  What the transfer path would carry is the reason this matters:
  flutter_secure_storage keeps session tokens in Keystore-backed encrypted
  preferences, which live in the `sharedpref` domain. The wrapping key is held
  in the Android Keystore and does not itself transfer, so what would move is
  ciphertext rather than usable credentials — but "the copy that left the device
  is useless" is a weaker statement than "no copy left the device", and only the
  second one is a control.

  The default is the wrong way round for this application. Per the same
  documentation, a missing section is not a neutral state: "If there are no
  rules for a particular backup mode, such as if the <device-transfer> section
  is missing, that mode is fully enabled for all content". Every mode is
  therefore declared, and every declared mode excludes every domain.

  Every domain in the documented set is listed explicitly rather than relying on
  `root` to imply the rest. `root` and `device_root` cover the internal storage
  roots; `external` is outside them; and the device-protected (Direct Boot)
  domains are a separate tree from the credential-protected ones. Listing all
  nine costs nothing and removes the question of which one subsumes which.

  <cross-platform-transfer> is declared for the same reason, and is safe to
  declare on older platforms: the framework's rules parser scans for the one
  section it is looking for and steps over every tag that is not it, so a
  section a given release does not recognise is skipped rather than rejected.
  That property is what makes the section additive at all — it ships in the same
  file to devices whose parsers predate it.

  Auto Backup's own always-excluded set (the no-backup and cache directories) is
  unaffected by any of this; it is excluded by the platform regardless.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </device-transfer>
    <!--
      CROSS-PLATFORM TRANSFER IS THE WEAKEST OF THE THREE SECTIONS, AND THIS
      COMMENT SAYS SO RATHER THAN IMPLYING OTHERWISE.

      `platform` is required on this element and on neither of the two above.
      Omitting it does not make the section permissive; it makes the resource
      invalid, and lintVitalRelease then fails with "Missing required attribute
      platform". That asymmetry hid a broken release build for several commits:
      a debug assembly parses this file happily and lintVitalRelease runs only
      on a release assembly, so the debug APK CI built passed throughout while
      a release APK could not be produced at all. CI now assembles a release
      artifact for exactly that reason.

      WHAT THE VALUE IS, AND HOW FAR THAT IS EVIDENCED. `ios` is used because
      it is the only cross-platform target the framework names anywhere:
      `BackupAgent.FLAG_CROSS_PLATFORM_DATA_TRANSFER_IOS`, documented as "a
      cross-platform transfer to or from iOS" (AOSP sources for android-37,
      `android/app/backup/BackupAgent.java:201`). An earlier revision of this
      file said `apple_icloud`, which was invented here and appears in no AOSP
      source and in no lint rule.

      The correction matters more than it looks, because NEITHER value is
      rejected. `FullBackup.java:735` reads this attribute as an opaque string
      and `:853` uses it directly as a map key; no enumeration of valid values
      exists in the installed platform sources or in
      `FullBackupContentDetector`. A wrong value therefore does not fail the
      build and does not fail at runtime — it silently addresses a platform
      nobody transfers to, and the section becomes inert while still looking
      correct. That is exactly the failure a green build cannot detect, so the
      value is asserted exactly rather than by pattern in
      platform_hardening_test.dart.

      NO `<platform-specific-params>` IS DECLARED, DELIBERATELY. That element
      carries `bundleId`, `teamId` and `contentVersion`
      (`FullBackup.java:108-110`), which identify the iOS counterpart an
      Android app's data may be matched TO. They exist to enable a match. No
      Apple Team ID is configured for this project, no iOS counterpart is
      published, and inventing one would put a fabricated identity into a
      shipping artifact. Their absence cannot widen anything: with no
      counterpart identified there is nothing for the subsystem to match, and
      every domain below is excluded regardless.

      WHAT IS THEREFORE NOT PROVEN. That this section actually closes an
      Android-to-iOS transfer on a device running the feature. The whole path
      is behind `Flags.enableCrossPlatformTransfer()`, no such device was
      available, and the exact attribute value could not be confirmed against
      an enumerated list because the platform publishes none. Cross-platform
      transfer is NOT claimed closed; it is a HIGH pre-release gate recorded in
      the phase report. `allowBackup="false"`, the cloud-backup section and the
      device-transfer section are not affected by any of this and are what
      actually holds today.

      No `path` attribute appears on any exclusion, and that is the documented
      whole-domain form rather than an omission: `FullBackup.java:1099-1102`
      handles a null path with the comment "Allow things like
      <include domain="sharedpref"/>", substituting the empty string, which
      resolves to the domain root.
    -->
    <cross-platform-transfer platform="ios">
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </cross-platform-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
